## Introduction to Matrix Operations in Java
This article introduces basic methods in Java for matrix operations, including addition, multiplication, inverse, transpose, and other relevant operations. The main functions are provided as static utility methods, and all methods are unit tested.

## Matrix Definition
A `Matrix` is a two-dimensional array of numbers, defined using three parameters: number of rows (`nrows`), number of columns (`ncols`), and the data as an array of `double` values.

```java
public class Matrix {
    private int nrows;
    private int ncols;
    private double[][] data;

    public Matrix(double[][] dat) {
        this.data = dat;
        this.nrows = dat.length;
        this.ncols = dat[0].length;
    }

    public Matrix(int nrow, int ncol) {
        this.nrows = nrow;
        this.ncols = ncol;
        data = new double[nrow][ncol];
    }
}
```

## Transpose of a Matrix
The transpose of a matrix is produced by swapping the rows with columns.

```java
public static Matrix transpose(Matrix matrix) {
    Matrix transposedMatrix = new Matrix(matrix.getNcols(), matrix.getNrows());
    for (int i = 0; i < matrix.getNrows(); i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < matrix.getNcols(); j++) {
            transposedMatrix.setValueAt(j, i, matrix.getValueAt(i, j));
        }
    }
    return transposedMatrix;
}
```

## Determinant of a Square Matrix
A square matrix has an equal number of rows and columns. The determinant of a square matrix can be calculated using the following method:

```java
public static double determinant(Matrix matrix) throws NoSquareException {
    if (!matrix.isSquare()) {
        throw new NoSquareException("Matrix needs to be square.");
    }
    if (matrix.size() == 1) {
        return matrix.getValueAt(0, 0);
    }
    if (matrix.size() == 2) {
        return (matrix.getValueAt(0, 0) * matrix.getValueAt(1, 1)) - (matrix.getValueAt(0, 1) * matrix.getValueAt(1, 0));
    }
    double sum = 0.0;
    for (int i = 0; i < matrix.getNcols(); i++) {
        sum += changeSign(i) * matrix.getValueAt(0, i) * determinant(createSubMatrix(matrix, 0, i));
    }
    return sum;
}
```

## Cofactor of a Matrix
The cofactor of a matrix `A` is matrix `C` where the value of element `C_ij` equals the determinant of a matrix created by removing row `i` and column `j` from matrix `A`.

```java
public static Matrix cofactor(Matrix matrix) throws NoSquareException {
    Matrix mat = new Matrix(matrix.getNrows(), matrix.getNcols());
    for (int i = 0; i < matrix.getNrows(); i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < matrix.getNcols(); j++) {
            mat.setValueAt(i, j, changeSign(i) * changeSign(j) * determinant(createSubMatrix(matrix, i, j)));
        }
    }
    return mat;
}
```

## Inverse of a Matrix
The inverse of a square matrix `A` is the matrix `A^-1` where `AA^-1 = I`. The inverse of a matrix can be calculated using the following method:

```java
public static Matrix inverse(Matrix matrix) throws NoSquareException {
    return (transpose(cofactor(matrix)).multiplyByConstant(1.0 / determinant(matrix)));
}
```

## Points of Interest
All of the above operations are fundamental in linear algebra, and the inverse of a matrix is one of the most complex operations to understand and implement.

